## What Is an SBOM?
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

A *Software Bill of Materials* is a formal, machine-readable inventory of all components used to build a software artifact.

Think of it like:

- **Canned food:** Ingredients list, nutritional info, best-before date, manufacturer
- **Software SBOM:** log4j@2.17.1, spring-core@5.3.20, vulnerability data, fixed-in version, supplier

## What's Inside an SBOM?
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<table>
<thead>
<tr><td><b>Field</b></td><td><b>Example</b></td></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Component name</td><td><code>golang.org/x/text</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Version</td><td><code>v0.3.7</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>License</td><td><code>BSD-3-Clause</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Supplier / Maintainer</td><td><code>golang.org</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Dependency relationship</td><td><code>my-app → rsc.io/quote → golang.org/x/text</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Checksum (hash)</td><td><code>sha256:aa0398e9...</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Known vulnerabilities</td><td>CVE-2023-XXXXX</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Standard formats: *SPDX* (ISO/IEC 5962), *CycloneDX* (OWASP)

## Why Does It Exist? — The Wake-Up Calls
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

Supply chain attacks have exploded since 2020.

A *supply chain attack* compromises an upstream component (library, build tool, distribution channel) to infect all downstream users.

.image images/Software-Supply-Chain-Integrity.png

## Timeline of Major Supply Chain Attacks
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre>2020 --- SolarWinds
        Attackers breached SolarWinds' build system.
        Malicious code injected into Orion updates.
        18,000 organizations infected, including US govt.

2021 --- CodeCov
        Bash Uploader script modified to exfiltrate
        credentials from CI/CD pipelines.

2021 --- Log4Shell (CVE-2021-44228)
        CVSS 10.0 - the most severe vulnerability ever.
        93% of enterprise cloud environments affected.
        Hundreds of millions of devices at risk.
        Exploit existed unnoticed since 2013.</pre>

## Timeline (cont.)
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre>2022 --- node-ipc / protestware
        Dependency overwritten with malicious code
        deleting files in Russia & Belarus.

2023 --- 3CX Supply Chain Attack
        Trading software compromised -> 3CX installer
        trojanized -> downstream customers infected.

2024 --- XZ Utils Backdoor
        Sophisticated multi-year social engineering
        planted a backdoor in SSH (narrowly caught).

2025 --- Bybit / Lazarus
        $1.5B crypto theft via supply chain compromise
        of safe{Wallet} multisig deployment process.</pre>

## Timeline (cont.)
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre>2026 --- Mini Shai-Hulud npm Worm (May-Jun)
        Self-replicating worm on npm stealing GitHub
        developer credentials. Affected GitHub, OpenAI, Mercor.
        187+ packages infected.

2026 --- DAEMON Tools Installer Backdoor (May)
        Official installers trojanized with backdoor
        via compromised build pipeline. Signed with
        legitimate certs. Highly targeted APT campaign.

2026 --- Vercel / OAuth Supply Chain Breach (Apr)
        Attackers breached Vercel via OAuth token theft
        from third-party integration provider,
        compromising downstream customer deployments.</pre>

## Why Does It Matter? — Zero-Day Response
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

*Without SBOM:* "We have Log4Shell somewhere... good luck finding it."

*With SBOM:* <code>grep log4j sbom.json</code> - instantly know:
- Which products are affected
- Which versions
- Where each component lives
- Whether it's reachable from attack surface

Log4Shell example: Companies *without* SBOMs spent *weeks* inventorying. Companies *with* SBOMs knew in *minutes*.

## Vulnerability Management
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

SBOM + vulnerability database = continuous monitoring:

<pre>SBOM ---> grype/govulncheck ---> "log4j-core@2.14.1 has CVE-2021-44228"
                                 "golang.org/x/text@v0.0.0-201709... has GO-2022-1059"</pre>

## License Compliance
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

- 96% of commercial codebases contain open-source
- 70-90% of a typical codebase is open-source
- SBOMs track every license: MIT, GPL, Apache, proprietary...

Avoid legal risk from license violations.

## Government & Regulatory Mandates
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<table>
<thead>
<tr><td><b>Mandate</b></td><td><b>Region</b></td><td><b>Requirement</b></td></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>US Executive Order 14028 (2021)</td><td>USA</td><td>"Provide an SBOM for any software sold to the government"</td></tr>
<tr><td>CISA SBOM Framework</td><td>USA</td><td>Minimum elements: supplier, component, version, hash, dependencies</td></tr>
<tr><td>EU Cyber Resilience Act</td><td>EU</td><td>Mandatory SBOM for digital products</td></tr>
<tr><td>NTIA "Minimum Elements"</td><td>USA</td><td>Baseline SBOM requirements</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## Government & Regulatory Mandates (cont.)
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<table>
<thead>
<tr><td><b>Mandate</b></td><td><b>Region</b></td><td><b>Requirement</b></td></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>CCCS Supply Chain Guidance (2022)</td><td>Canada</td><td>"The cyber threat from supply chains" — official guidance from the Canadian Centre for Cyber Security on software supply chain risks; aligns with international SBOM best practices</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

SBOMs are no longer optional. They are becoming a *legal requirement* for software procurement.

Canadian context: While Canada has no SBOM-specific law yet, the CCCS guidance sets the expectation for due diligence. Canadian companies selling to US/EU markets must comply with EO 14028 and the CRA. Several Canadian provinces are watching these frameworks closely.

## Trust & Transparency
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

- *Procurement:* "Show me your SBOM before I buy your software."
- *Vendors:* Publish SBOMs to prove you've done your homework.
- *Customers:* Import vendor SBOMs into your own vulnerability management.

