## SBOM Formats — Deep Dive
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

Three major formats dominate. Each has a different origin story, philosophy, and sweet spot.

<table>
<thead>
<tr><td><b>Format</b></td><td><b>Standard</b></td><td><b>Maintainer</b></td><td><b>First Published</b></td><td><b>Primary Focus</b></td></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>SPDX</td><td>ISO/IEC 5962:2021</td><td>Linux Foundation</td><td>2011</td><td>License compliance / general BOM</td></tr>
<tr><td>CycloneDX</td><td>OWASP standard</td><td>OWASP Flagship</td><td>2017</td><td>Security & vulnerability mgmt</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWID</td><td>ISO/IEC 19770-2</td><td>ISO / IEC</td><td>2015</td><td>Software identification & asset mgmt</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## SPDX — The ISO Standard Workhorse
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

*History:* Born in 2011 from the Linux Foundation's need to track open-source licenses. The *oldest* SBOM format. Became ISO/IEC 5962:2021. Latest version: SPDX 3.0.1.

*Original purpose:* License compliance - identifying every open-source license in a codebase.

*Today:* A general-purpose BOM format expanded far beyond software:

<table>
<thead>
<tr><td><b>SPDX Profile</b></td><td><b>What It Describes</b></td></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Software</td><td>Traditional software components, packages, files</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security</td><td>Vulnerabilities, CVEs, advisory references</td></tr>
<tr><td>Build</td><td>Build process metadata, build artifacts, SLSA provenance</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI / ML</td><td>AI models, datasets, training data, hyperparameters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dataset</td><td>Data packages and collections</td></tr>
<tr><td>Licensing</td><td>License expressions, copyrights (its original core)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## SPDX — Strengths & Weaknesses
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

*Strengths:*
- Mature ecosystem - 15+ years of tooling, validation, and adoption
- ISO status - de jure international standard for government procurement
- Broadcast scope - the only format with AI/ML profiles (critical for 2025+)
- License-first DNA - unmatched for compliance workflows
- Rich relationship modeling in SPDX 3.0

*Best for:* Government compliance, license auditing, multi-artifact ecosystems.

*Weaknesses:* Verbose XML/JSON, steep learning curve for full spec.

## SPDX — Example Snippet
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre><code class="hljs">{
  "spdxVersion": "SPDX-2.3",
  "dataLicense": "CC0-1.0",
  "packages": [{
    "name": "golang.org/x/text",
    "versionInfo": "v0.14.0",
    "licenseConcluded": "BSD-3-Clause",
    "externalRefs": [{
      "referenceCategory": "SECURITY",
      "referenceType": "cpe23Type",
      "referenceLocator": "cpe:2.3:a:golang:text:0.14.0:*:*:*:*:*:*:*"
    }]
  }]
}</code></pre>

## CycloneDX — Security-First, Purpose-Built
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

*History:* Created in 2017 by the OWASP community specifically for *application security*. OWASP Flagship Project. The fastest-growing SBOM format.

*Original purpose:* Security vulnerability tracking - which components have which CVEs.


<table>
<thead>
<tr><td><b>BOM Type</b></td><td><b>Purpose</b></td></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>SBOM</td><td>Standard software component inventory</td></tr>
<tr><td>SaaSBOM</td><td>Service BOM — APIs, endpoints, service deps</td></tr>
<tr><td>HBOM</td><td>Hardware BOM — firmware, chips, devices</td></tr>
<tr><td>ML-BOM</td><td>Machine Learning — models, datasets, weights</td></tr>
<tr><td>CBOM</td><td>Cryptography BOM — algorithms, key sizes, certs</td></tr>
<tr><td>OBOM</td><td>Operations BOM — infrastructure, deployment</td></tr>
<tr><td>VEX</td><td>Vulnerability Exploitability eXchange</td></tr>
<tr><td>BOM-Link</td><td>Cross-BOM references</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## CycloneDX — Strengths & Weaknesses
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

*Strengths:*
- Security-native - designed hand-in-hand with vulnerability databases
- Lightweight - simpler schema than SPDX, faster to parse and validate
- Best vulnerability tooling - native support in Grype, Trivy, Dependency-Track
- VEX built-in - attach exploitability analysis directly to the BOM
- Service awareness - unique ability to document API dependencies

*Best for:* Security teams, vulnerability management pipelines, container scanning, microservice architectures.

*Key difference from SPDX:*
SPDX is a *license-compliance format that added security.*
CycloneDX is a *security format that added license tracking.*

## CycloneDX — Example Snippet
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre><code class="hljs">{
  "bomFormat": "CycloneDX",
  "specVersion": "1.6",
  "components": [{
    "type": "library",
    "name": "golang.org/x/text",
    "version": "v0.14.0",
    "licenses": [{"license": {"id": "BSD-3-Clause"}}],
    "purl": "pkg:golang/golang.org/x/text@v0.14.0",
    "properties": [{
      "name": "aquasecurity:trivy:SHA256",
      "value": "aa0398e9681939a4e4208322563050730"
    }]
  }]
}</code></pre>

## SWID — The Enterprise Asset Tag
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

*History:* ISO/IEC 19770-2:2015. Designed by the *IT asset management (ITAM)* community, not developers.

*Original purpose:* Identify installed software on enterprise endpoints for inventory and license compliance.

*Strengths:*
- Installation-aware - tracks *which* software is *actually installed* on a machine
- Microsoft ecosystem - deeply integrated into Windows (MSI installers generate SWID tags)
- Enterprise procurement - used by SAM tools for license reconciliation
- Granular identification - tags per-installation, not per-package

*Weaknesses:* Poor at transitive deps, limited cloud-native tooling, XML-only, less community momentum.

## How They Fit Together
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre><center>
+--------------------------------------+
|            SBOM Ecosystem            |
|                                      |
|    SPDX <--> CycloneDX <--> SWID     |
|      |          |            |       |
|   License    Security    Asset Mgmt  |
|  Compliance   Vulns      Inventory   |
+--------------------------------------+
</center></pre>
<pre>
Tools like Syft/Grype support ALL formats:
  syft dir:. -o spdx-json
  syft dir:. -o cyclonedx-json
  syft dir:. -o spdx-tag-value
</pre>

## Which Format Should You Use?
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<table>
<thead>
<tr><td><b>If you care about...</b></td><td><b>Choose...</b></td><td><b>Why</b></td></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Government compliance</td><td>SPDX</td><td>ISO standard, referenced in EO 14028</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vulnerability management</td><td>CycloneDX</td><td>Best CVE tooling, VEX, CBOM</td></tr>
<tr><td>License auditing</td><td>SPDX</td><td>License-first DNA, 15+ years maturity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Enterprise asset inventory</td><td>SWID</td><td>MSI/Windows integration, SAM tools</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI/ML model governance</td><td>SPDX</td><td>AI Profile in SPDX 3.0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Container scanning / CI/CD</td><td>Both</td><td>Most tools output both formats</td></tr>
<tr><td>API dependency tracking</td><td>CycloneDX</td><td>SaaSBOM — unique to CycloneDX</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crypto inventory (post-quantum)</td><td>CycloneDX</td><td>CBOM — unique to CycloneDX</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## Which Format Should You Use? (cont.)
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

Pragmatic advice: Generate *both* SPDX and CycloneDX from your build pipeline:

<pre>syft dir:. -o spdx-json=sbom.spdx.json -o cyclonedx-json=sbom.cdx.json</pre>

The cost of generating both is near zero.

## SBOM in Go — Built-in Superpowers
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

Go has *first-class SBOM support baked into the toolchain.*

- `go.mod` -- Your dependency manifest
- `go.sum` -- Tamper-proof checksum database
- `go version -m` — SBOM embedded in every binary
- `debug/buildinfo` — Programmatic SBOM extraction
- `govulncheck` — Go-native vulnerability scanner
- Syft / Grype — Full SBOM generation and scanning

## go.mod — Your Dependency Manifest
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre><code class="hljs">module myapp

go 1.26.4

require (
    github.com/gorilla/mux v1.8.1
    golang.org/x/text v0.14.0
)</code></pre>

Every Go project already has a *partial SBOM* — it lists all direct and indirect dependencies.

## go.sum — Integrity Verification
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre>golang.org/x/text v0.14.0 h1:ScY5V9QhFnNN...=
golang.org/x/text v0.14.0/go.mod h1:12V0...=</pre>

*Purpose:* Tamper-proof checksums. Verify every download against the Go checksum database (sum.golang.org).

*This is supply chain security built-in.*

## go version -m — SBOM Embedded in Every Binary
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre><code class="hljs">$ go version -m myapp
myapp: go1.26.4
    path    github.com/myapp
    mod     myapp    (devel)
    dep     github.com/gorilla/mux    v1.8.1    h1:Tu5M...
    dep     golang.org/x/text         v0.14.0   h1:ScY...
    build   -buildmode=exe
    build   -compiler=gc
    build   GOARCH=amd64
    build   GOOS=linux</code></pre>

Every Go binary is *self-describing.* The dependency tree is embedded at compile time. No external SBOM file required.

## debug/buildinfo — Programmatic Access
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre><code class="hljs">package main

import (
    "debug/buildinfo"
    "fmt"
    "os"
)

func main() {
    f, _ := os.Open("myapp")
    defer f.Close()

    info, _ := buildinfo.Read(f)
    fmt.Println("Go version:", info.GoVersion)
    fmt.Println("Dependencies:")
    for _, dep := range info.Deps {
        fmt.Printf("  %s@%s\n", dep.Path, dep.Version)
    }
}</code></pre>

## debug/buildinfo — Output
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

Every Go binary carries its own *machine-readable SBOM:*

<pre>Go version: go1.26.4
Dependencies:
  github.com/gorilla/mux@v1.8.1
  golang.org/x/text@v0.14.0</pre>

## govulncheck — Go-Native Vulnerability Scanning
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre><code class="hljs">$ govulncheck ./...</code></pre>

<pre>=== Symbol Results ===
No vulnerabilities found.

=== Package Results ===
Vulnerability #1: GO-2022-1059
    Denial of service in golang.org/x/text/language
    Found in: golang.org/x/text@v0.0.0-20170915032832
    Fixed in: golang.org/x/text@v0.3.8</pre>

Key difference from generic scanners:
- Uses Go's own vulnerability database (vuln.go.dev)
- Tracks *reachability* — not just "this dep has a CVE" but "does your code actually call the vulnerable function?"
- Reduces noise dramatically

## Syft — Generate Full SBOM from Go Projects
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre><code class="hljs">$ syft dir:. -o spdx-json</code></pre>

Produces a full SPDX 2.3 document with every Go module, license, checksum, and CPE/PURL identifiers.

<pre><code class="hljs">$ syft dir:. -o cyclonedx-json</code></pre>

Same in CycloneDX format.

## Grype — Scan SBOMs for CVEs
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre><code class="hljs">$ grype dir:.</code></pre>

<pre>NAME               INSTALLED    FIXED IN  VULNERABILITY   SEVERITY
golang.org/x/text  v0.0.0-...  0.3.3      GO-2020-0015    High
golang.org/x/text  v0.0.0-...  0.3.8      GO-2022-1059    High</pre>

Works on the SBOM generated by Syft, or directly on the directory / container image.

## Docs: End-to-End SBOM Pipeline in Go
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

**1. Build your Go app**

<pre><code class="hljs">$ go build -o myapp .</code></pre>

**2. Extract built-in SBOM**

<pre><code class="hljs">$ go version -m myapp</code></pre>

**3. Generate SPDX SBOM**

<pre><code class="hljs">$ syft dir:. -o spdx-json > sbom.spdx.json</code></pre>

**4. Scan for vulnerabilities**

<pre><code class="hljs">$ grype sbom:sbom.spdx.json</code></pre>

**5. Use Go-native scanner**

<pre><code class="hljs">$ govulncheck ./...</code></pre>

## Docs: Embed in CI/CD
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<pre><code class="hljs"># .github/workflows/sbom.yml
- name: Create SBOM
  uses: anchore/sbom-action@v7
  with:
    format: spdx-json
    output-file: "${{ github.event.repository.name }}-sbom.spdx.json"

- name: Scan SBOM
  uses: anchore/scan-action@v7
  with:
    sbom: "${{ github.event.repository.name }}-sbom.spdx.json"
</code></pre>

## Challenges & Limitations
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Challenge</th>
    <th>Mitigation</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>SBOMs get stale</td>
    <td>Regenerate on every build</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Transitive dependencies</td>
    <td>Depth matters — include all</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>False positives</td>
    <td>Use reachability (govulncheck)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Format fragmentation</td>
    <td>Both SPDX & CycloneDX are valid</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Binary-only software</td>
    <td>Use container image scanning (Syft)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Developer friction</td>
    <td>Automate in CI/CD</td>
  </tr>
</table>

## Key Takeaways
.background images/golang_slide_blue.png

1. *SBOMs are the ingredient label for software* — know everything in your stack
2. *Supply chain attacks are the new normal* — Log4j, SolarWinds, 3CX, XZ Utils, and 2026's Mini Shai-Hulud worm & DAEMON Tools backdoor
3. *Governments are mandating SBOMs* — US EO 14028, EU Cyber Resilience Act
4. *Go has SBOM built-in* — go.mod, go.sum, go version -m, debug/buildinfo
5. *Free tools exist* — Syft, Grype, govulncheck, CycloneDX-gomod
6. *Automate or fail* — CI/CD pipeline integration is essential

